title the sweep target that was a test
prompt Keaton said run the caravan falsifier, then sweep lattice.
think Stated the rubric before sampling so the judgment could not be post-hoc: a reason names a failure or consequence the assert expression does not carry; a restatement says the expression again in English.
think Sampled twenty covered asserts by even stride across caravan's 110 real files rather than taking the first twenty, which would all have come from one file.
obs Sixteen or seventeen of twenty name a reason. The clearest: a redress always leaves its last byte for the upshot itself, above assert(at == max_redress_bytes - 1) -- it explains the minus one. A run always knows what program to spawn, above assert(self_path.len >= 1) -- it says what the path is for. Three or four restate, like the channel roster stays within its bound above count <= max.
obs The falsifier does not fire. 92.8% is about reasons rather than vocabulary, and the 80% reading holds.
obs One caveat the reading surfaced honestly: those reasons are written in Caravan's own coined words -- a beckoning run, a withdrawn quarrel, the silent sharing. To a reader who knows the room they are reasons; to a newcomer they are riddles. Gauge's first rule one layer down, and the same finding as the obscure-assembly note.
think Then opened lattice to sweep it and stopped at the first line. lattice/lattice.rye declares itself a selftest in its own module doc, builds to lattice/bin/lattice selftest, and names every function welcome_add, welcome_matmul, welcome_reshape. All 199 asserts check a computed result against a known answer.
obs My own bin keyed on the function NAME, so welcome_matmul read as a contract. 289 test assertions across lattice and lantern were filed as contracts, and that is what made lattice look like the densest target on the board. The file had been saying what it was in its own first line the whole time.
obs REDS %207 fixed one conflation and shipped with another still inside it. A correction feels finished at the moment it explains the fault it went looking for, which is the transferable half.
obs Corrected: the law reads 80%, the gap 4,242 across 546 modules. lattice and lantern leave the table. The real target is pond, holding 1,820 -- 43% of the whole gap in one room -- led by pond/apps/drawn_terminal.rye at 312 with none covered. glow 3.4% of 205, amphora 0.0% of 93.
obs Swept nothing. The instruction named lattice and lattice needs nothing; substituting a 312-assert file for it would be my judgment standing in for Keaton's word on a large piece of work.
